# The negation of an interval with one-limb bounds (milestone2.md, "Interval kernels", "One
# limb"): exact; the empty set (+inf, -inf) is its own negation. Bounded exactly when the operand
# is, which for a bounded operand is that every member of -X is within Omega. For every precision.
kernel:        interval_neg_1
input:         stored xl
input:         stored xu
output:        zl
output:        zu
requires:      interval xl xu
ensures:       encloses_interval zl zu -x x=xl,xu tight=exact bounded_when=abs_le(-x,omega) bounded_exactly
min_precision: 1
source:        the bounds negated and exchanged (Milestone 2 listing)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        proved
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   proved
ieee_theorem:  proofs/theories/IeeeInterval1.v interval_neg_1_correct
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   sign changes only, from the upper bound to the lower one; encloses_interval states what comes out for every canonical interval

zl = neg(xu)
zu = neg(xl)
